  {
   "id": 11,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    80,
    580
   ],
   "size": [
    850,
    410
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 10,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "PLANAR HOLE PATCH QUICKSTART\n\n1. The bundled example.png is selected so the workflow validates on a clean install. Choose your plate in Load Image.\n2. Queue once. Atlas Derive Relief Mesh outputs its literal topology-hole mask.\n3. Atlas Planar Hole Patch rejects frame/oversize regions first, then attempts eligible islands smallest-first so sky gaps cannot consume the fit budget.\n4. Accepted vertices are camera-ray/plane intersections with exact projective UVs and shared perimeter indices.\n5. Read PATCH REPORT for the exact accepted/rejected counts. REMAINING HOLES previews the literal output mask.\n6. In Atlas Viewport enable Project and orbit gently. Enable Matte to overlay the same remaining-hole mask.\n\nThe direct hole-mask wire is automatic mode. For deliberate silhouette repair, intersect it with a painted MASK before Atlas Planar Hole Patch."
   ],
   "color": "#25405c",
   "bgcolor": "#16283b"
  },
  {
   "id": 12,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    1590,
    500
   ],
   "size": [
    520,
    490
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 11,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "WHY THESE PATCH DEFAULTS\n\n• ring_cells = 2: uses the closest surrounding relief while retaining enough samples to stabilize small islands.\n• max_components = 512: covers dense 128-grid plates while unsafe frame/oversize regions remain outside the fit budget.\n• normal_tolerance = 22°: defines which surrounding normals agree for each island.\n• min_normal_support = 0.20: the agreeing cluster must represent at least 20% of valid surrounding normals, allowing one-sided facade evidence without accepting arbitrary directions.\n• The agreeing normals are averaged directly into the patch angle. max_plane_error = 0.30 m chooses/checks the densest local plane position when that angle appears at multiple depths.\n• max_patch_edge_factor = 20 rejects post-intersection spikes using each edge's source-pixel span × the island boundary's local metres-per-pixel. It is stable across image resolution and camera/world scale.\n• max_hole_fraction = 4% and enclosed_only = true retain the sky/frame safety gates.\n\nGeometry cannot invent hidden pixels, and frame-connected disocclusions still need a clean plate or scoped artist mask."
   ],
   "color": "#3b3156",
   "bgcolor": "#251d39"
  },
  {
   "id": 13,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    2140,
    500
   ],
   "size": [
    850,
    490
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 12,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "RETOPO + OPTIONAL WALL MERGE\n\nAtlas Retopologize Layer runs AFTER the planar patch, so the original relief and accepted planar patches are one mesh for decimation and UV regeneration. Decimate needs trimesh + fast-simplification; missing dependencies degrade softly and leave the patched solve unchanged.\n\nThe default viewport wire now comes DIRECTLY from Retopologize Layer so wall proxies cannot hide the patch result. The wall/merge branch remains as an optional disconnected experiment: max_objects = 0 prevents boxes/cubes, but Atlas Merge Geometry only appends wall primitives—it is not a boolean/weld and retopology does not fuse them."
   ],
   "color": "#4b3a24",
   "bgcolor": "#2e2215"
  },
